neovim9 — Neovim 0.12.4 for 9front (cc9). Changelog.
(`pac9 changelog neovim9` shows this; newest first.)

0.3.3 — 2026-07-26
  Resizing the window reaches nvim.

    - nvim took its size from $LINES/$COLUMNS, because a terminal here hosts it
      on a PIPE (Plan 9 has no ptys), so out_isatty is false and the TIOCGWINSZ
      step never ran. Those variables are frozen: fork is
      rfork(RFPROC|RFFDG|RFENVG), so the process holds a COPY of the environment
      group from exec. The SIGWINCH poller had the same problem — it watched
      /env/LINES and /env/COLS, which never change, so it never fired.
      Both now go through TIOCGWINSZ, which cc9 0.2.2 answers from the
      terminal's live size file.
      Measured on cirno, resizing 1024x768 -> 620x660 mid-session: 0.3.2 reports
      `&columns`/`&lines` = 113/45 (the startup size), 0.3.3 reports 68/38.
      Laying out at the old width is what put clipped lines and repeated rows
      on screen.

0.3.2 — 2026-07-26
  Relinked against the fixed cc9 runtime (see `pac9 changelog cc9` 0.2.1). Two
  things change, and both are in the runtime rather than in nvim:

    - A job whose exec fails no longer kills nvim's own threads. cc9's fork()
      handed the child a copy of the worker-thread registry, whose pids belong to
      NVIM, and a child that exits without exec'ing posted "kill" to all of them.
      nvim checks executable() first, so the reachable case is a file executable by
      mode that cannot be exec'd — and after one of those, the next job's output
      never arrived (jobwait returned -1 with the job invalid). Gate:
      test/jobgate.lua, run as `nvim --headless --clean -l test/jobgate.lua` —
      JOBGATE FAIL on 0.3.1, PASS here.
    - Everything is faster. The runtime shipped by cc9 0.1.0/0.2.0 was compiled at
      -O0 with byte-at-a-time memcpy/memset; nvim links that runtime, so every
      allocation, string operation and screen buffer copy paid for it.

  Verified on bare-metal cirno: `:!cat /env/user` inside alacritty9, then typed
  text still reaching the buffer afterwards.

0.3.1 — 2026-07-25
  Console follow-ups from real use over `drawterm -G`, plus the fix that lets
  the upstream test suite run.

    - The screen is restored on exit. A console publishes no $TERM, and the
      builtin terminfo nvim falls back to without one has no smcup/rmcup — so
      it drew onto the PRIMARY screen and had nothing to restore, leaving the
      editor's screen sitting above the shell prompt after :q. When $TERM is
      unset and stdout is a real console, it is now guessed as xterm-256color
      (the same thing nvim already does on Windows via os_tty_guess_term). An
      explicit $TERM still wins.
    - `--listen` no longer kills the editor. Plan 9 has no AF_UNIX and libuv's
      uv_pipe_bind is unported, so every --listen fails; upstream treats that
      as a user error and exits. Here it is a platform limit, so it degrades
      exactly as a failed autogenerated servername already did: logged, empty
      v:servername, startup continues.
    - Fixed silent output loss at exit in the cc9 runtime: exit() never closes
      fds, and the epilogue killed the write-ring drain threads before they
      ran, discarding bytes the caller had already been told were written.
      Flushed first now, with a one-second budget so a stalled peer cannot
      hang exit.

  With --listen non-fatal, Neovim's own functional suite runs on 9front for the
  first time: 577 tests passing across the api/core/vimscript/lua groups. The
  unit suite does not and will not — it drives nvim's C internals through FFI
  against a shared library this port does not build, and shells out to a C
  preprocessor at test time.

0.3.0 — 2026-07-25
  Runs on a bare console. Until now nvim needed a terminal emulator that sets
  $TERM (alacritty9); over `drawterm -G`, the physical console, or any plain
  console session it hung with a blinking cursor and never drew anything. Three
  fixes, two of them in the cc9 runtime underneath:

    - A real console is now recognised as a terminal. cc9 decided tty-ness from
      $TERM alone, which a console session does not set, so the TUI refused to
      start. It now also asks the kernel for the fd's path (/dev/cons).
    - Enter works. A Plan 9 console delivers NL where a terminal emulator sends
      CR, and nothing in between converts it, so every <CR> mapping went unfired
      — in netrw, pressing Enter on a file did nothing. Console input now
      translates NL to CR. (Ctrl-J is the cost, and was never distinguishable
      from Enter on a console anyway.)
    - Fixed a cc9 regression that made freshly built binaries paint nothing at
      all, in every terminal: on the exec path cc9 closed a private reader dup
      by fd NUMBER, after the child's spawn shuffle had already reused that
      number, so the TUI client closed its own --embed server's RPC pipe. The
      server exited, the client tore the UI down having written only its init
      sequence. Anything built against cc9 since 2026-07-20 was affected.

  Directory browsing got faster. netrw's listing rations the one stat per entry
  that only buys the '*' executable marker: it is ~95% of the cost of a listing
  on 9front (every stat is a 9P round trip), so above 500 entries the marker is
  traded for the seconds (`g:netrw_fastlist_statmax` tunes it). Wildcard
  expansion no longer stats each candidate twice.

  Same editor (upstream 0.12.4: LuaJIT interpreter, libuv, 7 treesitter parsers,
  jobs / :terminal / LSP).

0.2.0 — 2026-07-17
  First versioned release. Rebuilt against a correctness pass on the cc9 C
  runtime underneath it — stubs that used to return success while doing nothing,
  several of which an editor actually leans on:

    - Durable writes. The runtime's fsync used to return 0 without flushing, so
      swap/undo/shada files written before a crash could be lost. fsync is now a
      real 9P flush (honored by gefs), which is exactly what :w and crash
      recovery rely on.
    - Append mode is correct — log-style appends land at end-of-file every write.
    - errno is per-thread, so Neovim's async/libuv I/O reports errors reliably.

  Same editor (upstream 0.12.4: LuaJIT interpreter, libuv, 7 treesitter parsers,
  jobs / :terminal / LSP). Run it inside alacritty9 or any TERM-setting host.
  Earlier unversioned installs read as '-'; `pac9 upgrade neovim9` moves them here.
